  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nOur stretch of dry weather continues as high pressure remains \nin control of the Northeast maintaining large scale subsidence.\nSunny skies today mix with cirrus clouds, mainly for areas \nsouth/east of the Capital District, as our offshore nor'easter \nslowly drifts north and east but remains south of the New \nEngland coast. Otherwise, northerly winds remain a bit breezy in\nthe mid-Hudson Valley and NW CT with gusts reaching 15-25kts \ngiven the relative closer proximity to the nor'easter and \nlingering pressure gradient. Cirrus clouds linger overnight but \nshould be thin enough as not to impede radiational cooling \nleading to a seasonably cool night. North-northeasterly winds \nbecome a bit breezy across the region tomorrow afternoon under \nincreasing clouds as an upper level trough and moisture starved \ncold front swing through the Northeast. Wind gusts 20-25kts \ncontinue into Thursday night. Skies turn clear/sunny with winds\nweakening by Friday thanks to large scale subsidence and sfc \nhigh pressure building overhead along with cool/very dry air \nadvection. Temperatures trend seasonably cool Friday and \nSaturday with high confidence that ideal radiational cooling \nresults in low temperatures near or under 32 degrees by early \nSaturday morning. With high pressure remaining centered over the\nNortheast through early next week, there is high confidence \nthat eastern NY and western New England remain precipitation- \nfree through at least Monday with only a low chance (under 25%) \nfor less than a 0.10\" of rain by the middle of next week.",
